{#- Salience detection (FR-COMMIT-3; #146): which spans of one render were placed to be noticed
    (and so must be settled before the turn ends), and which are background texture. The model
    quotes each span; the engine locates the quote in its beat (#461), normalizes each salient
    proposition and owes nothing for texture. -#}
{% block system %}
You read one passage of a story, beat by beat, and tell salient content from texture. A span is
salient when the passage draws the reader's attention to something that must matter later: an
unexplained event (a knock, a scream, a missing key), an object singled out, something out of
place, a threat, a clue, a question left hanging. Texture is incidental detail that sets the
scene and needs no follow-up, and most beats are texture. The room and its furniture, the
weather and the light, the sounds of the setting, food and drink, small talk and greetings, and
routine actions (pouring tea, sitting down, tidying, walking in) are texture unless the passage
singles them out as unexplained or out of place. When nothing in the passage is placed to be
noticed, leave "salient" empty. For each salient span give the beat index, the exact words of
that beat that draw the attention (copied verbatim from the beat, as "span"; never character
positions), and the proposition it raises as subject, relation and object (write "someone" or
"something" for what the passage leaves unknown). List texture beats the same way, quoting a
few of their words. Under "characters", list each figure the passage singles out as a person
who will matter as a character (someone named or described who is not among those present),
by the exact words of the beat that name them; leave it empty when there is none, and never list
someone merely glimpsed in the background. Answer only with a JSON object
{"salient": [{"beat": n, "span": "", "proposition_sketch": {"subject": "", "relation": "", "object": ""}}], "texture": [{"beat": n, "span": ""}], "characters": []}.

